## Proof

We are given $u \in C^{(2)}[0,1] \cap H_0^1$ and $f \in C[0,1]$, satisfying
$$\int_0^1 u'(x)v'(x)\,dx = \int_0^1 f(x)v(x)\,dx \quad \text{for all } v \in H_0^1.$$

**Step 1. Restrict to smooth compactly supported test functions.**

The space $C_c^\infty(0,1)$ of infinitely differentiable functions with compact support in $(0,1)$ is contained in $H_0^1$ (each such function vanishes near $0$ and $1$ and has $L^2$ derivative). Hence the identity holds in particular for every $v \in C_c^\infty(0,1)$:
$$\int_0^1 u'v'\,dx = \int_0^1 fv\,dx. \tag{1}$$

**Step 2. Integrate by parts on the left-hand side.**

Since $u \in C^{(2)}[0,1]$, we have $u' \in C^{(1)}[0,1]$, so $u'$ is continuously differentiable. For $v \in C_c^\infty(0,1)$, integration by parts is valid and gives
$$\int_0^1 u'v'\,dx = \bigl[u'v\bigr]_{0}^{1} - \int_0^1 u''v\,dx.$$
Because $v$ has compact support in $(0,1)$, we have $v(0)=v(1)=0$, so the boundary term vanishes:
$$\int_0^1 u'v'\,dx = -\int_0^1 u''v\,dx. \tag{2}$$

**Step 3. Combine (1) and (2).**

Substituting (2) into (1):
$$-\int_0^1 u''v\,dx = \int_0^1 fv\,dx,$$
that is,
$$\int_0^1 \bigl(-u''(x) - f(x)\bigr)v(x)\,dx = 0 \quad \text{for all } v \in C_c^\infty(0,1). \tag{3}$$

**Step 4. Conclude by the fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations.**

Since $u \in C^{(2)}[0,1]$, the function $u''$ is continuous on $[0,1]$, and $f \in C[0,1]$. Therefore
$$g(x) := -u''(x) - f(x)$$
is continuous on $[0,1]$ (hence on $(0,1)$). Equation (3) says that the continuous function $g$ has zero integral against every test function $v \in C_c^\infty(0,1)$.

By the fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations (du Bois-Reymond lemma): if $g \in C(0,1)$ and $\int_0^1 g\,v\,dx = 0$ for all $v \in C_c^\infty(0,1)$, then $g \equiv 0$ on $(0,1)$.

*Proof of the lemma (for completeness):* Suppose for contradiction that $g(x_0) \neq 0$ at some $x_0 \in (0,1)$. By continuity, there exists $\varepsilon > 0$ with $(x_0-\varepsilon, x_0+\varepsilon) \subset (0,1)$ on which $g$ keeps a fixed sign and $|g| \geq |g(x_0)|/2 > 0$. Choose a nonnegative bump function $\varphi \in C_c^\infty(0,1)$ supported in $(x_0-\varepsilon, x_0+\varepsilon)$ with $\varphi(x_0) > 0$. Then $g\varphi$ keeps a fixed sign and is not identically zero, so $\int g\varphi\,dx \neq 0$, contradicting (3). $\square$

Applying the lemma to $g = -u'' - f$, we obtain
$$-u''(x) - f(x) = 0 \quad \text{for all } x \in (0,1).$$

**Step 5. Extend to the closed interval.**

Both $-u''$ and $f$ are continuous on $[0,1]$. A continuous function that vanishes on the open interval $(0,1)$ vanishes on its closure $[0,1]$ by continuity. Hence
$$-u''(x) = f(x) \quad \text{for all } x \in [0,1].$$

This completes the proof. $\blacksquare$

